diff --git a/wasm/README.md b/wasm/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..fe34342
--- /dev/null
+++ b/wasm/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,117 @@
+# ooRexx 5.3 WebAssembly / RxSock development port
+
+Baseline: ooRexx 5.3.0, trunk r13254.  Toolchain qualification: Emscripten 6.0.9.
+
+This development port compiles the ooRexx interpreter and API statically to
+WebAssembly, builds `rexx.img` with the WASM `rexximage` executable, embeds the
+image into the runtime MEMFS, and embeds the stock RxSock package into the
+interpreter.  RxSock keeps its Rexx API; Emscripten SOCKFS supplies a WebSocket
+transport for stream sockets.
+
+## WASM platform policy
+
+* no curses / orxncurses;
+* no child process spawning from ADDRESS handlers;
+* no external rxapi daemon: the API server (external data queues, function
+  and subcom registration, macrospace) is linked in and reached through an
+  in-process transport (`rexxapi/common/platform/unix/SysLoopbackConnection`);
+* user identity: Emscripten has no passwd database, so `getpwnam()` and
+  `getpwuid()` are wrapped to return the user the process runs as (the host
+  user under Node, the synthetic user `wasm` in the browser), for `USERID()`,
+  `cd ~user` and the RexxAPI;
+* static `rexx` (linked `--whole-archive`, so the `_rexx_init()` constructor
+  that creates the interpreter locks is kept) and `rexxapi`;
+* extension packages embedded in the interpreter (no dlopen):
+  rxsock, rxregexp, rxmath (registered in the image), hostemu and rxunixsys
+  (registered on first request) — see `PackageManager::embeddedPackages`;
+* the interpreter runs on a pthread (`-sPROXY_TO_PTHREAD`), so the JS main
+  thread stays free to start workers and deliver WebSocket events; a pool of
+  4 workers is pre-started (`-sPTHREAD_POOL_SIZE=4`);
+* no Asyncify: blocking is legal on the interpreter thread;
+* native WebAssembly exceptions in the standardized encoding
+  (`-fwasm-exceptions -sWASM_LEGACY_EXCEPTIONS=0`, exnref; the legacy one is
+  deprecated, and it makes WebKit before 26.6 miss C++ exceptions far more
+  often, see the comment in CMakeLists.txt): Chrome 137, Firefox 131,
+  Safari 18.4, Node 22 or later;
+* deep recursion raises Error 11 (Control stack full): besides the usual
+  linear-stack check, `Activity::checkStackSpace()` probes the JS engine's
+  native stack (where the frames really live) every few activation levels,
+  with a call that recurses through wasm frames, calibrated on each thread to
+  a quarter of its stack, so it works whatever the engine's frame and stack
+  sizes (a JS-only probe misses Firefox's wasm stack limit);
+* 8 MiB WASM stack, growable memory;
+* the wall clock (`TIME`, `.DateTime`, elapsed times) with sub-millisecond
+  resolution: `performance.timeOrigin + performance.now()`, where Emscripten's
+  `gettimeofday()`/`CLOCK_REALTIME` are `Date.now()`, whole milliseconds;
+* `rexx.img`, `socket.cls`, `streamsocket.cls` and `rxregexp.cls` preloaded
+  into MEMFS.
+
+Blocking `SockConnect`, `SockRecv`, and timed `SockSelect` preserve the native
+RxSock contract by polling Emscripten readiness with zero-time syscalls and
+sleeping between attempts (socket syscalls are proxied to the main thread).
+Nonblocking sockets preserve `EINPROGRESS` and readiness/SO_ERROR completion
+semantics. `wasm/emscripten-dns-proxy.js` makes Emscripten's fake-DNS lookup
+run on the main thread too, so host names resolve consistently.
+
+## Build
+
+With Emscripten 6.0.9 installed through emsdk
+(`./emsdk install 6.0.9 && ./emsdk activate 6.0.9`):
+
+    ./wasm/build-wasm.sh /path/to/emsdk/upstream /tmp/oorexx-wasm-build
+
+The first argument can also be an extracted official `wasm-binaries.tar.xz`
+(whose top level contains `install/`).  The runtime is built into
+`/tmp/oorexx-wasm-build/bin` (`rexx.js`, `rexx.wasm`, `rexx.data`).
+
+The initial CMake cache `cmake/wasm-emscripten-6.0.9-cache.cmake` records the
+qualified Emscripten libc capabilities and avoids slow generic Unix probes.
+
+## Node qualification
+
+For offline qualification Node 22 needs a `require('ws')` provider because
+Emscripten SOCKFS uses that API in Node. `wasm/node-ws-compat/index.js` adapts
+Node 22's built-in WHATWG WebSocket client for that purpose.
+
+The qualification server uses Python `websockets` and accepts the Emscripten
+`binary` WebSocket subprotocol:
+
+    ./wasm/tests/qualify-node.sh /tmp/oorexx-wasm-build
+
+It needs Python 3 with `websockets` (`python3 -m pip install websockets`;
+where pip refuses to change the system Python, PEP 668, as with Homebrew's,
+use a virtual environment: `python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate &&
+python3 -m pip install websockets`) and GNU `timeout` (on macOS:
+`brew install coreutils`; `gtimeout` is accepted too).
+
+Qualified behaviours include core Rexx execution, RxSock create/close,
+connect/send/recv, 5,000-byte partial stream reads, arbitrary binary strings,
+finite select timeout/readiness, nonblocking EINPROGRESS completion,
+connection refusal, DNS/localhost, and the unchanged `.StreamSocket` wrapper.
+
+## Browser status
+
+The generated `rexx.js/rexx.wasm/rexx.data` target `node,web,worker`. A pthread
+browser deployment must be served from a cross-origin isolated origin (COOP:
+`same-origin`, COEP: `require-corp`). `wasm/tests/http_isolated.py` is a minimal
+local server for that.
+
+Exercised in headless Chromium: core Rexx, START/REPLY/.Alarm and concurrent
+threads, SysSleep, RxSock and `.StreamSocket` over WebSocket, external data
+queue, rxregexp, rxmath, deep recursion (Error 11, at a depth set by the
+browser; see the limits below).
+
+## Known limitations / deliberate non-claims
+
+* Listening sockets are not supported (browser: Emscripten SOCKFS; Node: the
+  client-only `ws` shim makes `listen()` fail with EOPNOTSUPP).
+* UDP/datagram semantics are not qualified.
+* Native TCP servers need a WebSocket-facing endpoint/proxy; a real WebSocket
+  server can be addressed directly.
+* ADDRESS commands requiring an OS child process fail closed (RC 127).
+* Maximum recursion depth is set by the JS engine's native stack (internal
+  CALL / internal function calls / method calls): Node about 7,100 / 5,400 /
+  4,000; browser workers about 900 / 700 / 500 in Chromium, 1,300 / 900 / 700
+  in Firefox, 5,100 / 3,500 / 2,800 in WebKit.
+* Pthreads + growable memory carries an Emscripten performance warning; it is a
+  correctness-first development setting, not a final performance profile.
